\end{center}

For $n = 12$, $p = 2$, $a = 2$, $i = 4$: $\bin{12}{4} = 495$ is odd. For $p = 3$, $i = 3$: $\bin{12}{3} = 220$ is coprime to $3$. No prime survives, so $F(11) = 1$.

\section{Applications and Discussion}

The criterion $n \mid \bin{n}{i}$ for all interior $i$ characterizes primality; Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is the complete prime-power refinement. The hard half is purely a Kummer-carry computation, the same engine behind Lucas' theorem and the Sierpi\'nski structure of Pascal's triangle modulo $p$. A key indexing point: divisibility is governed by the base-$p$ representation of $n = k+1$, not of $k$.

\section{Future Directions}

Replacing the interior of one row by the stretched central family $\bin{qk}{k}$, $q = 2,\dots,k$, gives $F^\star(k) = \gcd_{2\le q\le k}\bin{qk}{k}$, related to OEIS A080170. Conjecturally: (1) $F^\star(k) = D(k)$ for $k \ge 3$; (2) $F^\star(k) = 1$ iff the largest prime-power component $P$ of $k+1$ has $P^2 < k+1$; (3) the $p$-exponent of $F^\star(k)$ is $\max(0, a - \lfloor\log_p m\rfloor)$ for $k+1 = p^a m$; (4) infinitely many two-sided counterexamples to the naive ``$k$ prime power'' guess (via Dirichlet); (5) stability under deeper truncation $F^\star_t$.

\section{Conclusion}

The gcd of the interior of row $n = k+1$ is a perfect prime-power detector: $1$ iff $n$ is not a prime power, and the underlying prime $p$ when $n = p^a$. The proof combines a uniform divisibility stamp with a carry-free base-$p$ split, illustrating how Kummer's carry theorem renders an opaque gcd transparent.

